{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "import math\n\ndef closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Returns the closest integer to a numeric string, rounding away from zero for equidistant numbers.\n    '''\n    number = float(value)\n\n    floor_val = math.floor(number)\n    ceil_val = math.ceil(number)\n\n    dist_to_floor = abs(number - floor_val)\n    dist_to_ceil = abs(number - ceil_val)\n\n    if dist_to_floor < dist_to_ceil:\n        return int(floor_val)\n    elif dist_to_ceil < dist_to_floor:\n        return int(ceil_val)\n    else:  # Equidistant, e.g., X.5\n        # Round away from zero: for positive numbers, round up; for negative, round down.\n        if number >= 0:\n            return int(ceil_val)\n        else:\n            return int(floor_val)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "import math\n\ndef closest_integer(value):\n    \"\"\"\n    Takes a string representing a number and returns the closest integer to it.\n    If the number is equidistant from two integers, rounds it away from zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    f_value = float(value)\n\n    # Handle numbers that are already integers or 0.0 directly\n    if f_value == int(f_value):\n        return int(f_value)\n\n    floor_val = math.floor(f_value)\n    ceil_val = math.ceil(f_value)\n\n    # Calculate distances to the floor and ceil integers\n    dist_to_floor = f_value - floor_val\n    dist_to_ceil = ceil_val - f_value\n\n    # If equidistant, apply the \"round away from zero\" rule\n    if math.isclose(dist_to_floor, 0.5) and math.isclose(dist_to_ceil, 0.5):\n        if f_value > 0:\n            return int(ceil_val)  # e.g., 14.5 rounds to 15\n        else:  # f_value < 0\n            return int(floor_val) # e.g., -14.5 rounds to -15\n    else:\n        # Otherwise, return the integer that is strictly closer\n        if dist_to_floor < dist_to_ceil:\n            return int(floor_val)\n        else:\n            return int(ceil_val)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "def make_a_pile(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, you have to make a pile of n levels of stones.\n    The first level has n stones.\n    The number of stones in the next level is:\n        - the next odd number if n is odd.\n        - the next even number if n is even.\n    Return the number of stones in each level in a list, where element at index\n    i represents the number of stones in the level (i+1).\n    \"\"\"\n    pile = []\n    current_stones = n\n    for _ in range(n):\n        pile.append(current_stones)\n        current_stones += 2\n    return pile"}
