{"task_id": "HumanEval/163", "solution": "def generate_integers(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two positive integers a and b, return the even digits between a\n    and b, in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    start = min(a, b)\n    end = max(a, b)\n    \n    result = []\n    for num in range(start, end + 1):\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            result.append(num)\n            \n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "def is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is the product of exactly three prime numbers.\n    The number 'a' is expected to be less than 100.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _generate_primes(limit):\n        \"\"\"Generates a list of prime numbers up to a given limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\"\"\"\n        sieve = [True] * (limit + 1)\n        primes = []\n        for p in range(2, limit + 1):\n            if sieve[p]:\n                primes.append(p)\n                for multiple in range(p * p, limit + 1, p):\n                    sieve[multiple] = False\n        return primes\n\n    def _factorize(n, primes):\n        \"\"\"Finds all prime factors of a number n from a given list of primes.\"\"\"\n        factors = []\n        temp_n = n\n        for p in primes:\n            if p * p > temp_n:  # Optimization: if current prime squared exceeds remaining n, break\n                break\n            while temp_n % p == 0:\n                factors.append(p)\n                temp_n //= p\n        if temp_n > 1:  # If temp_n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n            factors.append(temp_n)\n        return factors\n\n    # Generate primes up to the maximum possible value 'a' can take, which is 99.\n    # A limit of 100 ensures primes up to 97 are covered.\n    all_primes = _generate_primes(99)\n    \n    # Factorize the input number 'a' using the generated primes.\n    prime_factors = _factorize(a, all_primes)\n    \n    # Return True if the number of prime factors is exactly 3.\n    return len(prime_factors) == 3"}
